From bb6b4ed739cc2dd8f66d71e031ffb9d2af95a5c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carter Blair <cartergblair@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 18:55:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] feat: balance diversity arms into near-equal thirds

The 2.5:1 low-to-high allocation maximized how far the budget could push the
high target, but measured r* (where the achievable ceiling starts binding) is
~0.3-0.5, so most of the low arm bought no contrast while the high arm ran at
2 groups. Balanced thirds is the conservative choice under noisy measurement:
D-optimal for a quadratic dose-response, minimax under unknown per-arm outcome
variance, robust to losing a group per arm to fallback, and still ~1.55x the
SNR of 5/13/2 on the ceiling-capped linear contrast (~85% of the endpoints-only
optimum).

Extreme targets are pulled in by TARGET_PULL_IN (5% of the achievable range)
on both ends, replacing the one-sided ceiling margin, so assignment error is
roughly symmetric instead of strictly inward.
